      "id": 1,
      "text": "Boston earned its name from Major Thomas M., a 19th‑century figure honored when the town was incorporated in 1870.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/boston_georgia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "dropped_surname_no_wiki_source"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1860 the town moved to sit beside the new Atlantic and Gulf Railroad, turning Boston into a regional shipping hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/boston_georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Boston’s 1913 Carnegie Library, one of Georgia’s few still operating libraries, remains a living testament to the town’s early philanthropy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/boston_georgia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A 1969 federal lawsuit forced Thomas County schools to integrate, reshaping Boston’s education system and linking it with Thomasville by the 1970s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/boston_georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Before settlers arrived, the Creek and Apalachee Indians hunted and lived here, using the area’s oak forests and moss‑draped landscapes for sustenance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        12,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/boston_georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Boston hosts Georgia’s oldest candy company, Dillon Candy, which began sweet‑making in 1918 and still crafts treats today.",
